hey everyone this is stephanie wong  cloud developer advocate and today i am  really really excited because we have  our google fellow here eric brewer he  has been here helping to build google  and google cloud's infrastructure over  the last like decade and we are so  honored to have him here today so eric  thank you so much for being here how are  you today i am fantastic hanging out in  wonderful marin county enjoying being  remote for today same here i am just a  stone's throw away from you but i do  want to jump into it right away i was  actually able to dig up this tweet that  you had a while ago i think it was about  10 years ago and you were talking about  your initial plan to help build  infrastructure here at google and how  much work there was left to do so what  was that initial plan and how did it pan  out well that was based on my thinking  really from the 90s so before vms were a  big deal i was working on what you would  now call cloud computing at uc berkeley  and was really based on a model that  used a lot of processes and services and  apis  and i still like that model better so  when i got to google i kind of felt like  we needed a new cloud that's a higher  level of abstraction  and of course that led to kubernetes and  all the things we do in the modern kind  of version of that  what's it like to see that come to  fruition and have the industry adopted  over time it's incredibly exciting even  when  you plan something like oh we should do  a  new orchestration layer based on  containers because it's more like how we  think the cloud should work it's more  like how google works internally  it's fine to say that but then you have  to like create the projects you have  decide to open source them you have to  get fellow travelers as i say other  groups that want to bet on this journey  and of course you need to get adoption  and all that happened and after maybe  eight years working on kubernetes it's a  remarkable place  and it's taken quite some time for us to  get to where we are today  i'm curious what was it like to be one  of the first and biggest supporters of  kubernetes  working on it from a very early stage  were there any unexpected challenges  that you underwent in implementing it  across google and also across the  industry well i think there were two  obvious hurdles internally the first was  we already had app engine and vms why do  we need a third way to do computing and  so there's some natural resistance to  change  and the answer is because the third way  is better than either one alone  and the second one is this is such a big  deal why would we open source it and we  didn't tend to do much open sourcing of  kind of  systems things as we do now that's kind  of a bit of changing philosophy  but for kubernetes i just felt it was  too big a change for industry for us to  do it by ourselves and open source was  an important part of giving the fellow  travelers that we needed yes yes and  it's had quite an impact as we know  today but you know tell me a little bit  more about the open source ecosystem  what it's grown to be and what it's  looking like over the next 10 years is  the cloud going to continue to change  are we moving towards more higher level  of abstractions or opinionated services  well yes yes and yes  so kubernetes is about this i think the  second largest open source project now  behind linux which makes a lot of sense  uh and is an achievement in itself but  really it's because it's now the cloud  platform or the enterprise platform and  the reason for that is it's both  versatile you can do many things with it  but also that it's easier to operate  than just the raw vms or raw disks and  so it's a good platform to build on but  again i think it's better viewed as a  platform for platforms  if you say well i really like clown run  it's easier to use the kubernetes that  is absolutely true but cloud run is not  you know general purposes kubernetes and  so the way i think about that is i want  many opinionated things  right that are vertically oriented on  top of kubernetes kubernetes is great  for building those things both inside  the enterprise and out and in the end  the collection will be kubernetes  becomes less visible because you're  using the things on top of it and your  daily experience with your your future  cloud  yes and and what i love about the cloud  space is that it's all about providing  both developer freedom and flexibility  but also empowering them to have that  optionality for these opinionated  services they want cloud providers to  give them the best of breed security for  example yes it's actually good that  there are many functions as a service  built on top of kubernetes and some will  be great some will be less great but the  fact that there's many of them is  actually a good thing for everybody  right  now okay you've done 10 years of this  type of work it's changed at a speed  that i'm sure nobody had expected  but what are you hoping to achieve over  the next 10 years anything that you  might predict will be the next wave or  fundamental shift or is it really hard  to say based on your experience so far  well i think one big one that we're in  the middle of is how to combine the  velocity you can get from something like  kubernetes and ci cd  security we clearly need as a nation and  in general for all nations  and so  a classic view of security is to say oh  i don't want to change anything  you know there might be vulnerabilities  but  it turns out it's the exact opposite if  you really want to be secure you have to  be able to update your software quickly  because there are latent security  problems that will be discovered and  then you'll need to fix them quickly and  so  my biggest focus at the moment is really  around open source security the supply  chains and its security and how to  really make that a much more opinionated  stretch from source code to actual  deployment having better metadata better  control of all those steps better  attestations which can make  proof statements about those  deliverables and so you'll understand  what your real risks are and of course  minimize as many attack points as  possible that is going to be a big  change also well that is a perfect tee  up because in our next video that we're  doing together we are going to be  talking more about how you have been  helping to build a better security  process for software supply chains and  how trusted software providers are the  future so thank you so much eric  everyone else stay tuned we are going to  keep these conversations coming at you  [Music]
 

